  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1149 AM MST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nRAIN, STORMS, MOUNTAIN SNOW, AND GUSTY WINDS TODAY:\n\nA longwave trough and an associated cold front are moving across\nthe West, bringing a line of rain showers, thunderstorms and\nhigh elevation snowfall. This precipitation reached eastern Utah\nthis morning and will move across the region from west to east\nthrough the day. A few thunderstorms embedded in the line of \nprecipitation could be on the stronger side, particularly along\nthe Utah and Colorado border and Four Corners region during the\nafternoon hours today thanks to a bit of instability and strong\nwind shear. Stronger storms could bring brief periods of heavy \nrainfall, which may introduce some risk of flash flooding in \nflood-prone areas. \n\nForecast snow totals from this system are generally around 1-4 \ninches, with the greatest amounts favoring the highest mountain \npeaks. Most of the expected snow will fall during the evening \ntoday. Today's snowfall could create hazardous driving \nconditions, including blowing snow, over mountain passes so be \nsure to use caution if traveling. \n\nStronger wind gusts are ongoing across our region today. Wind \nAdvisories remain in effect for some valley areas in \nsoutheastern Utah and southwestern Colorado through 10 PM this \nevening where gusts up to 45 to 50 mph are possible. Tomorrow, \nweaker winds are expected as the trough propagates to our east. \n\nNEXT SYSTEM FRIDAY AND SATURDAY: \n\nForecast models continue to show another system arriving late \nThursday night through Saturday morning. Models still show an open \nwave setup. Precipitation totals continue to vary by model run, with \nthe central Colorado mountains being favored for the most snow\nin the latest runs. Differences among the models with regards \nto strength and timing keep confidence low at the moment. Most \nof the moisture is expected to arrive ahead of a cold front, \nmaking timing critical. Below freezing overnight low\ntemperatures could drop snow levels to some valley floors \nFriday night into Saturday morning. For now, however, the \nspecifics remain uncertain.",
